{"id":"7f28eebb-dfae-4739-b476-04a69764ee60","object":"chat.completion","created":1793257740,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"All three files' full texts and key mechanisms are in hand. Let me view the `envs` class attribute section of environ.py to match naming conventions.\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", 150, 221)['content'])\n```","reasoning_content":"I have all mechanisms. Now let me verify baseline was done (baseline_done: 1). Changed files none. Now implement.\n\nPlan: update plan statuses, then apply edits.\n\nFirst, environ.py: add a factory method `int_or_none_min`? Task says \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\". I'll add a static method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 32) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value!r}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nError message contains \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\" because __get__ wraps with self.name. But empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError \"'': invalid literal for int()\" — wrapped with name prefix? The __get__ catches Exception and does `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For ValueError, type(e)(\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\") works. Good.\n\nBut careful: the wrapping `try: raise type(e)(...) except TypeError: raise e from None` — ValueError accepts single arg, fine.\n\nNow add to envs class:\n\n```python\n    LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: int | None = EnvVar.int_or_none_min()\n```\n\nHmm, but need attribute named after the env var; __set_name__ sets name. So class attr name must be exactly `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Let me check what envs class looks like — I saw lines 95-221 earlier but not in this context. Need to know attribute naming convention. Let me read environ.py lines 180-221 to see envs class attribute names.\n\nActually I need to see existing attributes. Let me read the envs class region. But note \"Do not repeat full reads to register them again\" — read_range on new lines is fine.\n\nlogger.py: NewLineFormatter gains `max_message_chars` keyword-only. Validate: must be int >=32, bool excluded, None allowed.\n\nImplementation: override format partially: we need record.message computed (via super().format which sets record.message? Actually logging.Formatter.format calls record.getMessage() and sets record.message). We must not mutate record.msg/args. Approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)  # this sets record.message = record.getMessage()\n        ...\n```\n\nBut record.message is set on the record — that's fine (standard logging behavior), and doesn't modify msg/args. The budget should apply to message body only, excluding prefix, exc_info, stack_info. Simplest: truncate record.message before super().format? That would mutate record.message (not forbidden; only msg/args/exc_info/stack_info). But then multi-line prefix code uses record.message; also other handlers may use record.message — but standard Formatter.format always recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() at each call, so mutation is harmless-ish. Still, safer: compute replacement outside.\n\nPlan:\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if not self._should_truncate(record):\n            return self._format_full(record)  # existing logic\n        original_message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate(original_message, self.max_message_chars)\n        # temporarily set record.message? \n```\n\nSimplest robust approach: temporarily set record.message to the truncated text, call super().format(record)... but super().format recomputes record.message = record.getMessage(). Hmm. logging.Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\n...\n```\n\nSo record.message is always recomputed from getMessage(). So to influence it without touching msg/args, we must override formatMessage:\n\n```python\n    def formatMessage(self, record):\n        message = record.message\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        return super().formatMessage(record)  # uses record.message though\n```\n\nlogging.Formatter.formatMessage does `return self._style.format(record)` using record.message via mapping. So I need to temporarily substitute record.message:\n\n```python\n    def formatMessage(self, record):\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            return super().formatMessage(record)\n        original = record.message\n        if not isinstance(original, str):\n            return super().formatMessage(record)\n        replaced = _truncate_message(original, budget)\n        if replaced == original:\n            return super().formatMessage(record)\n        record.message = replaced\n        try:\n            return super().formatMessage(record)\n        finally:\n            record.message = original\n```\n\nBut the existing format() does:\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nif \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n    prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n    msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\nAfter format, record.message is restored to full original message (because finally). The multi-line prefix uses `msg.find(record.message)` — original message not found in truncated msg → find returns -1 → msg[:-1] prefix which is wrong. So I should keep record.message as truncated during the multi-line processing in format(), then restore.\n\nBetter: handle truncation in format() itself:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            return self._format_with_prefix(record)\n        original_message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate_message(original_message, budget)\n        if truncated == original_message:\n            return self._format_with_prefix(record)\n        record.message = truncated\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n            if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n                prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n                msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n            if self.use_color:\n                color = _LEVEL_COLORS.get(record.levelno)\n                if color:\n                    msg = f\"{color}{msg}{_COLOR_RESET}\"\n            return msg\n        finally:\n            record.message = original_message\n```\n\nBut super().format(record) recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() — full message. Damn.\n\nOptions: temporary swap of record.msg? Forbidden (\"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args\").\n\nAlternative: implement format without super().format: replicate standard formatter pipeline manually using truncated message. Use `self.formatTime`, `self.formatMessage`? formatMessage uses record.message via style. We could build the whole string ourselves: \n\n```python\ns = self.formatMessage(record_with_truncated_message)\n```\n\nHmm, could create a shallow copy of the record via `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` and set copy's message... Standard: `copy = logging.LogRecord(...)`. Easier: use `copy.copy`? Not imported; could import copy — allowed standard library. Then `record = copy.copy(record)`; set copy.message. But super().format still recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() from msg/args of the copy which are the full ones. Unless we modify copy.msg... copy is not shared, so modifying copy.msg is safe. But also `record.__dict__` copied, exc_info etc. Set copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = () — safe on the copy. But requirement says not modify shared original; copy is fine.\n\nHmm, but simpler: override formatMessage to handle truncation and set record.message temporarily, and in format() do multi-line processing differently:\n\nApproach: keep format() as existing, but have it operate using a truncated message for prefix computation. Let me restructure:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n        if budget is None or keep_full:\n            return self._format(record)\n        original = record.message  # may not be set yet\n        ...\n```\n\nCleanest: make format build the message via formatMessage override plus computing prefix from truncated message stored in an instance-local variable? Instance is shared across threads potentially — not thread safe.\n\nUse a record copy approach but only when truncation needed:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, budget)\n            if truncated != full_message:\n                shadow = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n                shadow.msg = truncated\n                shadow.args = ()\n                shadow.message = truncated\n                text = super().format(shadow)\n                if \"\\n\" in text and shadow.message:\n                    prefix = text[: text.find(shadow.message)]\n                    text = text.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n                if self.use_color:\n                    color = _LEVEL_COLORS.get(record.levelno)\n                    if color:\n                        text = f\"{color}{text}{_COLOR_RESET}\"\n                return text\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nWait `logging.makeLogRecord` sets created, msecs, etc. and re-derives message? makeLogRecord(dict) does `rv.__dict__.update(dict)`. Then super().format sets rv.message = rv.getMessage() = str(truncated) since args=() → truncated. Good. exc_info is preserved in dict; formatException used → traceback appended after truncated message. Requirement: budget only limits message body, not exception traceback — satisfied since we only truncate the message portion and traceback appended by super().format.\n\nstack_info: preserved too and appended; fine.\n\nBut note: `logging.makeLogRecord` copies `asctime`? Not present yet. Fine.\n\nAlso `exc_text` might be cached—fine.\n\nNow truncation function:\n\n```python\n_OMISSION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMISSION_MARKER\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return marker[:budget]  # ? budget>=32 so marker 15 < budget\n    head_len = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail_len = remaining - head_len\n    head = message[:head_len]\n    tail = message[len(message) - tail_len:] if tail_len > 0 else \"\"\n    return f\"{head}{marker}{tail}\"\n```\n\nBudget 32 → marker len 15, remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. Matches spec.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(...)\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nSpec says \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". Should we require int only? What about float 64.0? Raise ValueError. Use isinstance(bool) check.\n\n__init__ signature: `def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None)`.\n\nDefault logger init: `fmt = NewLineFormatter(_FORMAT, datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT, use_color=..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)`. Need import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — but does logger importing environ cause circular import? environ imports only stdlib. logger → environ ok.\n\nWait, existing code imports? Check logger imports: no deepinfer import at top. Add `from deepinfer import environ` and use `environ.envs.LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Hmm, actual envs attribute name — need to check environ.py envs class. I earlier read 95-221 but content shown in previous turn (not in this context). I need to see envs class attributes to know naming convention and where to add. Let me read 150-221 now.\n\nStats logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nAlso requirement: \"初次读取后缓存，只修改系统环境变量不应立即改变\" — descriptor handles.\n\nIf the env var parsing raises during `_setup_logger()` at import when invalid value set... test might set invalid and re-init expecting ValueError. Fine.\n\nBut careful: `_setup_logger()` is called at import; if env var invalid at import, import fails. Tests probably set valid. But maybe tests set env var 16 then reinit expecting ValueError after refresh. Since env cached from import (when unset → None), rearm then `_setup_logger` would raise ValueError. Fine.\n\nLet me read envs class attributes region."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":7819,"completion_tokens":2968,"total_tokens":10787,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2911},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":3723},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}